integrated_rules:
When evaluating whether alternative energy can replace fossil fuels within a tight net-zero timeline, ask whether the proposal credibly closes *both* the reliability bottleneck and the demand/sector bottleneck. 

If (i) the low current share of renewables (e.g., ~20% renewable electricity in the U.S.) is plausibly due to underinvestment, permitting delay, and fossil incumbency rather than a hard physical limit, so that political will/capital allocation can scale non-fossil power faster; **and** (ii) the transition includes strong energy-efficiency and demand-reduction measures plus electrification of end-uses such that total required generation (and thus required firm/long-duration backup) drops; **and** (iii) the plan delivers enough *firm, dispatchable, low-carbon* capacity to cover the full spectrum of demand (peaks, seasonal lows, and periods of extreme low renewable output) as well as hard-to-electrify sectors (e.g., aviation/long-haul freight and key industrial heat/process needs) so fossil molecules are not still needed; **and** (iv) if nuclear is used as the quickest land-efficient firm option, there is credible evidence that deployment can avoid major-accident “setbacks” (e.g., Fukushima-scale outcomes) that would negate the carbon-free benefit; 

then conclude that fossil fuels can be phased out (i.e., alternatives can effectively replace them). Otherwise—if efficiency does not eliminate the need for firm/long-duration balancing during low-output periods, if firm low-carbon capacity cannot scale quickly enough, and/or if hard-to-electrify sectors still require fossil molecules—then conclude that a *natural-gas bridge* using existing infrastructure is the practical interim route to cut emissions without risking reliability failures, until non-fossil firm capacity and sectoral alternatives are sufficient for true replacement.
